  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 27,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.066,
            0.374,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.12,
            0.882,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "fractional unit. In this case, the common denominator can be made "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 \\times 4 = 12,  i.e."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , we can find equivalent fractions with fractional unit "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 1 2 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.177,
            0.827,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us write the equivalent fraction for each given fraction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.212,
            0.478,
            0.243
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_27_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1 \\times 3}{4 \\times 3} = \\frac{3}{12}, \\quad \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1 \\times 4}{3 \\times 4} = \\frac{4}{12}."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.258,
            0.766,
            0.288
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 } { 1 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 4 } { 1 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " have the same fractional unit, i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 1 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.298,
            0.518,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } \\quad + \\quad { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } \\quad = \\quad { \\frac { 3 } { 1 2 } } \\quad + \\quad { \\frac { 4 } { 1 2 } } \\quad = \\quad { \\frac { 7 } { 1 2 } }   ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.336,
            0.887,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This method of addition, which works for adding any number of fractions, was first explicitly described in general by Brahmagupta in the year 628 CE! We will describe the history of the development of fractions in more detail later in the chapter. For now, we simply summarise the steps in Brahmagupta’s method for addition of fractions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.479,
            0.665,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s method for adding fractions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.511,
            0.885,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Find equivalent fractions so that the fractional unit is common for all fractions. This can be done by finding a common multiple of the denominators (e.g., the product of the denominators, or the smallest common multiple of the denominators)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.606,
            0.885,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Add these equivalent fractions with the same fractional units. This can be done by adding the numerators and keeping the same denominator."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.679,
            0.669,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Express the result in lowest terms if needed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.711,
            0.806,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us carry out another example of Brahmagupta’s method."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.744,
            0.533,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ": Find the sum of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{2}{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 5 } } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.781,
            0.884,
            0.827
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The denominators of the given fractions are 3 and 5. The lowest common multiple of 3 and 5 is 15. Then we see that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.836,
            0.466,
            0.868
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_27_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2 \\times 5}{3 \\times 5} = \\frac{10}{15}, \\quad \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{1 \\times 3}{5 \\times 3} = \\frac{3}{15}"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
